------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PARITY APPROACH (web → SwiftUI)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The web page is a thin `PageContainer` wrapper (title + subtitle) around the shared
`<FeatureToggles />` surface. The native parity composes the already-shipped P4
`FeatureToggles` feature view (apps/apple/TeslaSync/feature-views/FeatureToggles*.swift)
inside a titled, adaptive ScrollView scaffold:
  • Header  : TSPageTitle(featureConfig.title) over a muted subtitle(featureConfig.subtitle)
  • Body    : FeatureToggles(model:) — owns loading / empty / error / stale / offline /
              content states (no state is hidden behind a blank region; ADR-011).
  • Nav     : AppRoute.teslaFeatures (path /tesla-features), hosted via the
              AppRouteHostRegistry the shell renders; deep-link parses through
              AppRouteParser (segment match) — covered by tests.
Data sources: per the parity manifest the PAGE has none of its own ("renders from
navigation values / local state"); the embedded surface binds through its own P1/S8
seam (FeatureTogglesSource). The page's default `TeslaFeatureConfigAccountSource`
resolves the genuine pre-fetch empty state (web "No feature config data yet. Click
Refresh to fetch from Tesla.") and services Refresh; the shared KMP feature-config
store swaps in through the same seam once AppContainer.bootstrap lands (the deferral
VehicleSettingsModel already documents). No stubs, no hardcoded data.
Units/i18n/a11y: all copy via the catalog (String(localized:) / LocalizedStringKey);
SI handled inside the embedded surface; design tokens only (Color.TS / Font.TS /
TSSpacing / TSRadius); accessibility traits on the title + combined header.

